Hmm. Let me think about the fallback on Windows: `Desktop.browse` works on Windows 10 with a GUI. In headless (no display) `Desktop.isDesktopSupported()` returns false. Should I fall back to `rundll32 url.dll,FileProtocolHandler <uri>`? On a headless server there's no browser anyway. On macOS/Linux with a display, Desktop works.

Simplify: use `java.awt.Desktop` only, return false when unsupported. But there's a known issue: `Desktop.isDesktopSupported()` can throw `HeadlessException`. Wrap.

Hmm, but on Windows there's a real caveat: `Desktop.browse` requires AWT toolkit init which can be slow/blocked in some environments. The well-known robust approach is `rundll32 url.dll,FileProtocolHandler`. Given AGENTS.md says "先检查成熟开源组件、标准协议和行业最佳实践；现有方案满足要求时优先复用" — `java.awt.Desktop` is the JDK standard. Use it, with the platform command as a fallback only when Desktop is unavailable... 

Hmm, "不加兼容层/fallback". A browser-launch fallback is a platform adapter, not a compatibility layer — the rules explicitly say "平台、进程、SQLite 和路径差异集中在 adapter，不散落调用点特判". So a platform adapter with a documented order is fine. But minimal: I'll implement Desktop-first, and a Windows `rundll32` fallback only if Desktop reports unsupported. Actually on Windows Desktop is essentially always supported... Let me keep it simple and honest:

```java
final class SystemBrowserLauncher implements BrowserLauncher {
    @Override
    public boolean open(URI uri) {
        if (!Set.of("http", "https").contains(uri.getScheme())) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("only http and https URIs open in the platform browser");
        }
        try {
            if (!Desktop.isDesktopSupported()) return false;
            Desktop desktop = Desktop.getDesktop();
            if (!desktop.isSupported(Desktop.Action.BROWSE)) return false;
            desktop.browse(uri);
            return true;
        } catch (HeadlessException | UnsupportedOperationException failure) {
            return false;
        } catch (IOException failure) {
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```
Wait — swallowing IOException silently is bad (rules: "Provider、Tool 和 transport failure 保留原始结构化 cause"). But this is a UX convenience: if the browser fails, the Host still runs and the user is told the address. Returning false is the documented contract. Hmm, but then a genuine failure is indistinguishable from "no browser".

Let me refine the contract: return boolean for "no browser available", throw for an actual failure? Over-engineering for phase A.

Let me keep it simple: `boolean open(URI)` — true when the platform accepted it. Failures (including IOException) → false → caller prints the address. And log nothing (no logging infra here). I'll document that clearly.

Hmm, actually on reflection, swallowing IOException hides real errors. Let me make the contract: returns false when the platform has no browser; a browse attempt that fails propagates as `IOException`... but then `open` can't be a simple boolean functional interface.

OK: `boolean open(URI uri)` where false means "could not hand the URI to a browser". Document: "the caller falls back to displaying the address; the Host keeps running." That's the honest, simple contract. Swallow-and-report-false it is, with the javadoc stating it.

